h FG 163 mg/dL.  \nBut insulin wasn\'t measured then.  \nThen after starting Exenatide, the insulin is -26. But again, not sure if fasting.  \nWait, the note mentions "insulin (−26uIU/ml)" in the context of "cardiometabolic parameters including FG (−17mg/dl), insulin (−26uIU/ml), and TG (−12mg/dl)".  \nThe parentheses might indicate that these are changes from previous values.  \nSo maybe the insulin here is a delta from before, not the actual value.  \nSo perhaps the actual fasting insulin isn\'t provided.  \nThat would make calculating HOMA-IR impossible because we need both fasting glucose and fasting insulin.  \nBut wait, maybe there\'s another source of insulin data.  \nLet me check the initial preoperative evaluation again.  \nThe labs there include HbA1c 10.7%, FG 210 mg/dL, and 2-hour glucose 378 mg/dL.  \nBut insulin isn\'t listed here.  \nThen after starting Exenatide, the insulin is -26.  \nMaybe the insulin level before starting Exenatide was higher? But without knowing the exact value, we can\'t compute HOMA-IR.  \nAlternatively, maybe the insulin level of -26 is a typo or misunderstanding.  \nBecause negative insulin levels don\'t make sense.  \nInsulin levels are usually reported in uIU/mL, and negative numbers aren\'t typical unless there\'s a measurement error.  \nSo perhaps that\'s a mistake.  \nIf the insulin level is actually 26 uIU/mL, then maybe that\'s the value needed.  \nBut the note says "insulin (−26uIU/ml)", which could mean a decrease of 26 from baseline.  \nBut without knowing the baseline, we can\'t calculate HOMA-IR.  \nThis is confusing.  \nThe problem is that the necessary data (fasting insulin) isn\'t provided in the note.  \nUnless there\'s another part of the note I missed.  \nLet me read through again carefully.  \nThe note mentions "insulin (−26uIU/ml)" in the context of cardiometabolic parameters.  \nThe parameters listed are FG decreased by 17, insulin decreased by 26, and TG decreased by 12.  \nSo maybe the insulin here is a