Right, because albumin affects the anion gap by binding to some anions, so correcting for it gives a more accurate picture of the underlying electrolyte imbalance.  
First, I need to recall the formula for the anion gap.  
The standard anion gap is calculated as (Na+) - (Cl- + HCO3-).  
Then, the albumin-corrected anion gap adjusts for the albumin level.  
The formula for correction is: (4.0 - [albumin in g/dL]) * 0.3.  
So, if the albumin is below normal, you subtract that value multiplied by 0.3 from the original anion gap.  
If it's above normal, you add it.  
Wait, but albumin is usually around 3.5-5.0 g/dL.  
