Oh, that's way below normal.  
So the correction would be (4.0 - 1.5) * 0.3 = 2.5 * 0.3 = 0.75.  
Then subtract that from the original anion gap.  
But wait, do I have the original anion gap? Let me look back at the lab results.  
The serum chemistries list bicarbonate at 30 mEq/L, chloride at 102, sodium at 139.  
Wait, but let me double-check. Albumin-corrected AG is calculated as AG minus (4 - albumin) * 0.3. Yes, because each gram decrease in albumin contributes 0.3 mEq/L to the anion gap.
